Consider the function $f(z) = overline(z) = x - i y$. Which statement correctly describes its differentiability in the complex plane?

#pagebreak()
#set page(width: a_width, margin: a_margin)
#set text(size: a_size)
$f'(z)$ does not exist at any point in $CC$.

#pagebreak()
#set page(width: a_width, margin: a_margin)
#set text(size: a_size)
$f'(z) = 1$ everywhere in $CC$.

#pagebreak()
#set page(width: a_width, margin: a_margin)
#set text(size: a_size)
$f'(z)$ exists only along the real axis $y = 0$.

#pagebreak()
#set page(width: a_width, margin: a_margin)
#set text(size: a_size)
$f'(z)$ exists only at the origin $z = 0$.

